# The Forge - a farrier, a horse that is not right, and a sale on Saturday.
# Written for the library. One person, one craft, and the question of who a job is done for.

title: The Forge
start: start

== start ==
bg workshop_bench
music room_tone
The forge is at the end of a lane and it has been there longer than the lane has been
made up. You shoe for eleven farms and two riding schools and one man who keeps a
horse because his father kept a horse.
You are good at it. That is not a boast, it is the whole of what you have: you can
hear a hoof on tarmac from the yard and tell which foot it is.
-> the_van

== the_van ==
The van comes at ten with a horsebox behind it and a man you have not seen before, and
the horse comes down the ramp and stands in the yard with its weight on three legs and
its near hind toe just touching the ground, which is a thing a horse does when it does
not want to put weight on a foot.
You have seen it before you have said hello.
-> the_look

== the_look ==
man: He's for sale on Saturday. New shoes on him and he'll fetch what he's worth.
The horse is nine and it is not worth much and the man knows both of those things.
He wants the near hind shod. He wants it shod today, and he wants the shoes to look
new in a photograph.
* Pick the foot up and look properly. -> look_properly
* Say the price and get the tools. -> the_knowing
* Ask him when the horse went lame. -> ask_when

== look_properly ==
You run your hand down the leg and the horse flinches at the fetlock and does not
flinch at anything else, and when you lift the foot it comes up and the sole is hot
through the hoof wall, which you can feel with the flat of your hand.
There is a pulse in the digital artery that should not be there. You put the foot down
gently, which is not a thing you would normally do.
-> the_knowing

== ask_when ==
you: When did he go lame?
man: He's not lame. He's got a bit of a foot.
you: When did he get the bit of a foot?
man: A fortnight. He's been out on the grass.
A fortnight is long enough for an abscess to track and it is not long enough for the
hoof to have grown out, and both of those are things you can check and neither is a
thing he can argue with.
-> the_knowing

== the_knowing ==
You are holding a foot that is hot and pulsing and you are standing in your own yard
with your own tools on the bench, and the man is talking about the photograph.
Whatever happens in the next ten minutes is going to be a thing you did, and it is
going to be done to a horse that cannot say anything about it.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
* Shoe it. He is selling it either way and it is not your horse. -> shoe_it
* Refuse, and say why, out loud, in the yard. -> refuse
* Shoe it and tell the man nothing, then ring the buyer. -> tell_buyer
* Put the tools down and tell him to bring it back in a fortnight. -> fortnight

== shoe_it ==
You trim and you fit and you nail on four shoes that would photograph well, and the
near hind is the neatest of the four because you took trouble over it, and that is the
part you will think about.
The horse stands for it. Horses do. The man pays in cash and puts it back in the box
and the box goes up the lane and you sweep the yard and put the tools away.
-> ending_shot

== refuse ==
you: I'm not shoeing this.
man: You what?
you: The foot's hot. There's a pulse in it. He's got an abscess in there and if I nail
a shoe on it today you'll have a horse that can't walk by Wednesday and you'll have
sold it to somebody by Saturday.
He says a number of things. You let him say them, standing in your own yard, and then
he puts the horse back in the box and drives off up the lane too fast for the surface.
-> ending_refused

== tell_buyer ==
You shoe the horse. Then you take the man's money and you write the buyer's name down
off the advert he left on the bench, which he did not mean to leave, and you ring the
number that evening.
It is a woman with a field and a daughter and she listens to all of it and asks you two
questions, both of which are good ones, and then she thanks you and rings off, and you
have no idea what she does next.
-> ending_rang

== fortnight ==
You put the foot down and you pick the tools up off the bench and you put them back in
the box, which is a sentence in this yard and everybody who has ever stood in it knows
it.
you: Bring him back in a fortnight. If he's still lame then, I'll do what needs doing
and I'll do it properly. If he's not, I'll shoe him and you'll get your photograph.
The man stands there for a moment working out whether he has been refused, and decides
that he has not, and takes the horse away.
-> the_fortnight_after

== the_fortnight_after ==
He comes back in eleven days with the horse walking level and an abscess that broke on
its own out in the field, and you shoe him and it takes an hour and the shoes are good
and the photograph is taken in the yard by the man's son.
You do not say anything about the eleven days. He does not either. It is in the book
that the horse came in twice, which is all the book is for.
-> END

== ending_shot ==
The horse sells on Saturday. You see it go past the end of the lane in the box of a
dealer from the next county, and you know the dealer, and you know where that horse is
going, and you are the only person in this story who knows all three of those things.
You have shod four thousand horses. You can hear a hoof on tarmac from the yard and
tell which foot it is, and you could have told it from the ramp, and you did not.
-> END

== ending_refused ==
He rings the next day and says a number of things and you let him say them down a phone
this time, and then he rings the day after that and asks whether you will look at it on
Thursday, which is the only apology this trade has.
You look at it on Thursday. It is an abscess and it needs a vet and you say so and he
takes it to one, and on Saturday it does not sell, and the following Saturday it sells
to the woman with the field, and you shoe it for her for six years.
-> END

== ending_rang ==
You never find out. That is the whole of it: you did a thing and it went out of your
yard and into somebody else's life and you have no way of knowing whether it was the
right thing, and you go on shoeing for eleven farms and two riding schools and one man
who keeps a horse because his father kept a horse.
Three years later a woman stops at the end of the lane and says her daughter's horse
is called Bruno, and you say you do not remember him, and you do, and you are not sure
why you said that.
-> END
